# 🛡️ Custom RBAC System - STRICTLY READ-ONLY

## Tables
- `permissions`
- `permission_role`
- `roles`
- `admins`

## Middleware
- `CheckRolePermission`: compares route name vs permission string

## Bypass
- `AdminType::SUPER_ADMIN` skips all checks automatically

## Exclusions
- `exceptedRoutesFromRoles()` array in middleware

## 🚫 NEVER
- Modify the RBAC tables
- Replace with external packages (e.g., spatie/laravel-permission)
- Refactor the existing permission logic
- Suggest alternative permission systems

## ✅ ALWAYS
- Route name MUST exactly match permission string
  - Example: `admin.users.index` → permission `admin.users.index`
- If unsure about permission logic → STOP and ask for clarification

## Permission check placement

- Permission checks belong in the `CheckRolePermission` middleware — do not scatter `if (in_array(...))` or role checks across controllers, services, policies, gates, or Blade.
- Route-to-permission mapping follows the actual convention: the route name *is* the permission string (e.g. `admin.users.index` matches `'admin.users.index'` stored in `permission_role`).
- Display labels for permissions live in `lang/{locale}/admin/routes.php` — update that file when adding new route permissions.
- Policies and gates are for record-level or ownership authorization only. Do not use them to enforce or duplicate admin route permissions.

## Exception routes

Exception/override routes live in `app/Traits/Route/RouteTrait.php`:
```php
public static function exceptedRoutesFromRoles(): array
```
These bypass permission checks for all non-super-admin roles. Only add routes here after explicit security review.

## New routes

- Any new `admin.*` route should have a corresponding permission string or a documented bypass in `exceptedRoutesFromRoles()`.
- When adding a new resource to `routes/admin.php`, also add its route entries to `lang/{locale}/admin/routes.php` so they appear in the permission assignment UI.

## Review checklist

When auditing RBAC, verify:
- Every `admin.*` route in `routes/admin.php` has a matching entry in `lang/{locale}/admin/routes.php`.
- Every permission string in the database is prefixed with `admin.` (middleware filters by this prefix).
- No hardcoded role checks (`$admin->type === 'super_admin'`) exist outside the middleware — the middleware centralizes this logic.

## ⚠️ CRITICAL
If any code change involves permissions, roles, or admin access control → STOP and request explicit confirmation before proceeding.